{31} When He mentioned Noah and his people and how He destroyed them, He said: {Then We produced after them another generation}. The apparent view is that they were Thamud, the people of Salih, peace be upon him, because this story resembles theirs.

{32} {So We sent among them a messenger from themselves}: of their own kind, whose lineage, nobility and truthfulness they knew, so that this would be quicker to bring them to submission when he was one of them, and further from their aversion. He called them to what the messengers called their nations to: {"Worship Allah; you have no god other than Him."} All of them agreed on this call, which is the first call with which they summoned their nations: the command to worship Allah, the information that He alone deserves it, the prohibition of worshipping anything besides Him, and the information that this is false and corrupt. Therefore He said: {"Will you not then fear [Allah]?"}, that is, fear your Lord, so that you would avoid these idols and images.

{33} Then {the eminent ones of his people who disbelieved and denied the meeting of the Hereafter, and whom We had given luxury in the worldly life} said. That is, the leaders who combined disbelief and obstinacy, denial of the Resurrection and recompense, and whom their affluence in the worldly life had made arrogant, said in opposition to their prophet, in denial and in warning against him: {"This is not but a human like you, who eats of what you eat and drinks of what you drink."} That is, of your own kind. So what is it that makes him superior to you? Why was he not an angel who does not eat food or drink drink!

{34} {And if you obey a human like yourselves, indeed you would then be losers.} That is: if you follow him and make him your leader while he is like you, then you are indeed deprived of reason and will regret what you have done! This is strange, for loss and regret truly belong to the one who does not follow him and does not submit to him. Ignorance and great foolishness belong to the one who is too proud to submit to a human whom Allah has singled out with His revelation, favored with His message, and who is tried by the worship of trees and stones. This is similar to their saying: {"Shall we follow a single human from among us? Indeed we would then be in error and madness. Has the Reminder been sent down to him from among us? Rather, he is a liar, an insolent one."}

{35–36} When they denied his messengership and rejected it, they denied what he brought concerning resurrection after death and recompense for deeds. They said: {"Does he promise you that when you have died and become dust and bones, you will be brought forth? Far, far is what you are promised!"} That is, what he promises you of resurrection after you have been torn apart and become dust and bones is very far-fetched. They looked with a limited view and regarded this as impossible in relation to their own powers. They measured the power of the Creator by their own powers, exalted is Allah, so they denied His power to revive the dead and attributed to Him utmost incapacity. They forgot their first creation, and that the One who brought them into being from nothing, His restoring them after decay is easier for Him, and both are easy for Him. Why then do they not deny their first creation and obstinately contradict what is perceptible, saying: "We have always existed," so that their denial of resurrection would stand and they would move on with them to argue for the existence of the Mighty Creator? Here is another proof: that the One who revived the earth after its death is indeed the one who revives the dead; indeed He is over all things Powerful. There is yet another proof, which is what He answered those who denied resurrection in His saying: {"Rather, they wondered that a warner from among themselves came to them, and the disbelievers said, 'This is a strange thing. When we die and become dust, that is a far return.'"} He said in answer to them: {"We know what the earth diminishes of them"}, that is, through decay, {"and with Us is a preserved Book."}

{37} {"There is not but our worldly life; we die and live"}, that is, some people die and others live, {"and we will not be resurrected."}

{38} {"He is not but a man in whom there is madness."} Therefore he brought what he brought of the oneness of Allah and affirmation of the Return! {"So wait for him for a time."} That is: withhold punishment from him, by killing or otherwise, out of respect for him and because he is a madman who is not held accountable for what he says. That is, according to their false claim there was no longer any dispute with him over the soundness of what he brought, for they had claimed its falsehood. What remained was only whether they would harm him or not. By their assumption, their sound minds required sparing him and leaving him alone despite the existence of the justification for harming him! Is there any greater extent of obstinacy and disbelief than this?

