Commentary of Ibn Sa'di (Taysir al-Karim al-Rahman) on surah 23, verses 73–74:

{73–74} In these noble verses, God, exalted is He, mentioned every cause that necessitates faith, and He mentioned the obstacles and explained their corruption one by one. Among the obstacles He mentioned: that their hearts are in heedlessness (ghamrah), that they did not contemplate the Word (al-qawl), that they followed their fathers, and that they said, "There is madness (jinnah) in him" (about their messenger), as the discussion of these has already passed.

He also mentioned the matters that necessitate their faith: contemplating the Qur'an, receiving God's blessing with acceptance, knowing the state of the Messenger Muhammad (ﷺ) and the perfection of his truthfulness and trustworthiness, that he does not ask them any reward for it, and that his striving is only for their benefit and welfare, and that what he calls them to is a straight path (sirat mustaqim), easy for those who act upon it because of its uprightness, leading to the intended goal with nearness, a upright and tolerant monotheism (hanifiyyah samhah): upright (hanifiyyah) in monotheism (tawhid), tolerant (samhah) in practice. So your calling them to the straight path necessitates that whoever desires the truth follow you, because it is something that sound minds and natural dispositions (fitar) testify to as good and in agreement with the welfare of people. Then where will they go if they do not follow you? For they have nothing that suffices them and spares them from following you, because they are {away from the path}: deviating, avoiding, and swerving from the road that leads to God and to the abode of His honor. In their hands there is nothing but misguidance and ignorance. Thus it is with everyone who opposes the truth: he must be deviant in all of his affairs. God, exalted is He, said: {But if they do not respond to you, then know that they only follow their own desires. And who is more astray than one who follows his desire without guidance from God?}

